The '''Galwa national football team''' represents [[Galwa]] in men's international association football. It is governed by the Galwa Football Federation and is one of the principal western Burawi sides, drawing on the football culture of [[Hargiesa]], [[Bejol]], and the other major cities of the western kingdom. Galwan personal names often pair Burawi given names with city-derived surnames based on old urban centres such as Hargiesa, Bejol, and Hargan. Galwa opened their [[2026 AFA World Cup|2026 World Cup]] campaign with a 2–1 victory over [[Sanrobonia national football team|Sanrobonia]].

== History ==

=== Early development ===
Galwan football developed around the court cities and port centres of the western kingdom, especially [[Hargiesa]] and [[Bejol]]. The country's footballing culture is strongly Burawi in language and style, with clubs and supporters favouring compact organisation, physical intensity, and technical passing sequences built around local academy systems.

=== Modern era ===
In the modern era, Galwa have emerged as a disciplined and difficult side to break down. Their first appearance at the [[AFA World Cup]] in 2026 marked an important milestone for the kingdom's sporting profile, and the opening-day win over Sanrobonia immediately established the team as one of the more competitive newcomers to the tournament.

== Stadium ==

Galwa play their home matches at [[Hargiesa National Stadium]] in [[Hargiesa]], the kingdom's principal football venue. Important matches are also occasionally staged in [[Bejol]], [[Hargan]], and [[Varaan]].

== Playing style ==

Galwa are generally described as a compact, tactically organised side that blends Burawi technical quality with a more direct western coastal temperament. The team tends to defend in a disciplined block, work the ball through midfield, and rely on set pieces and quick transitions when opportunities appear.

